Subject: journal the superblock

ext4_sb_write() puts a superblock change on the medium by itself, with no
relation to any transaction. For fields like s_last_orphan that is exactly
wrong: the value only means anything together with the inode change it points
at, and a crash between the two -- either order -- leaves a state where one
happened without the other. The measured cost: with two entries on the orphan
chain, four consecutive cut points stranded an inode that no list, no
directory, and no bitmap scan would ever find again.

Three pieces make the superblock a journaled block:

  * ext4_sb_write_trans(): update the superblock's home block through the
    cache and mark it dirty in the current transaction. The change commits
    atomically with everything else in the transaction or not at all.
    Replay was already prepared for this, unreachably: the commit path tags
    a journaled block by number, block 0 tags as 0, and recovery has had a
    dedicated tag-0 branch restoring bytes 1024..2047 all along -- written
    for a writer that never existed until now. On 1 KiB volumes the
    superblock is block 1 and replays as an ordinary block. The Linux
    kernel replays both forms; measured across every cut of an orphan-add
    transaction, fourteen kernel recoveries, all clean.
  * ext4_sb_write() itself becomes cache-coherent: it still writes the
    medium directly, and now also folds the bytes into the cached copy of
    the superblock's block when one exists (ext4_bcache_update_if_cached,
    non-dirtying). Without this, the seven direct callers -- mount, unmount,
    journal start/stop, recovery -- would race a cached block 0 and a later
    journaled update would commit stale bytes over theirs.
  * jbd_recover() reloads the superblock from the medium after replay. The
    tag-0 branch refreshes the in-memory copy; the ordinary-block path on
    1 KiB volumes only writes the disk, and everything that runs after
    recovery -- flag edits, orphan cleanup, the unmount write-back -- was
    operating on a stale superblock and quietly undoing what replay had
    restored. Measured: a replayed orphan-list head vanished at exactly
    this seam and stranded its inode.

Callers choose the form explicitly; the journaled write is not a fallback.
Volumes without a journal keep the direct write and its immediate
durability, which is the best that mode offers.
